WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
This is Jeopardy!

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:26.340
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.700
I'm your host, Michael Esposito, and joining me are our two contestants.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:32.180
I'm Sean.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:33.740
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.940
And we have a bit of a special episode today.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:42.720
We've decided to take a, what should be a relatively easy game to discuss, Jeopardy!

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.460
on the NES, and we're going to turn it into an actual game of Jeopardy!

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:47.620
How do you guys feel about that?

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.200
Sounds fun. It seems too complicated.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.160
We're going to fail.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.920
Yeah, I feel like even though this is going to translate really well for us,

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.820
the listeners at home at some point are going to be like,

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.760
what? Why are they talking about this now?

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:03.900
They're like, huh?

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.040
But we'll see what happens.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.260
Chapters are dead. Yeah, there's no chapters for this one, that's for sure.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.520
But there will be a winner, and there will be no prize.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.920
I just want to get that out of the way.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.360
I have no money to give.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.580
We barely make any money on this show to begin with.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:28.840
It's true. But one thing I wanted to clear up is that instead of doing our usual developer things,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.200
or talking about the music and stuff, we're going to hop around a lot,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.920
because there's a bunch of different categories that we're going to go through,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.460
similar to a real game of Jeopardy!

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.020
So you're really going to want to listen to the entire episode if you're at all interested in any of this stuff.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.160
And I think you'll find some fun things in there too.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.120
So you guys ready to play?

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.820
Let's do it. Who goes first?

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.000
Alright, so I think we'll have, since Sean always introduces himself first,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.260
Joe will go first.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.580
And Joe, the categories, just so you know for this round, are

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:06.000
Play the Game, Trust Your Mechanic, Developing Situation, Get Nostalgic, and I Am 8-Bit.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:11.220
Yes, I'll take Play the Game for $400.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.420
Alright, this is Play the Game for $400.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.920
A plan of action or policy designed to achieve a major or overall aim?

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.420
Yeah, I got nothing.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.420
Alright, we were looking for what is strategy?

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.840
And that is Webster's definition of strategy.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.900
And this would be a great time for us to talk about our strategies when playing Jeopardy!

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.780
here. So guys, what did you do to best the computers in Jeopardy?

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:46.880
What I did usually is I would only buzz if I was pretty sure I knew.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.380
Because, you know, like in regular Jeopardy!

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.860
it does subtract points.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:58.480
And sometimes you'll get the computer to buzz in and they'll get it wrong.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.520
And the way that they do it in this game is they don't like guess something else.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:10.140
They just type in computer gibberish but with like some of the letters from the actual answer.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.660
So it was an interesting way to go about that.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.800
I don't know what you guys did, yeah.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:21.640
Yeah, I think using that a little bit, like if I don't know something,

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:27.000
kind of definitely taking advantage of the fact that they will type in parts of the answer.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.420
Which I think was their way to show like, oh look, they guessed wrong but like got some of it right.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:37.160
It's a little, it's a little like, not, doesn't translate exactly to how it would be in real Jeopardy!

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:44.660
But it's definitely, definitely helps you get answers that you didn't get on your first read of the question.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:53.560
Yeah. And I think one thing that I stole from is the James Holzhouser strategy of playing Jeopardy!

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.620
Where you play through the bottom first.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:02.880
So you go for the 500 questions in each category to try to get, like to see if you know them and can make the most money off the bat.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:10.640
Because if you get the 1500 right there, at least in the first round, it's going to be very hard for anybody else to make up the difference.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.860
True, but you could also put yourself in a huge hole.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.920
But I guess that's why smart people play this.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:21.960
Yeah, and that's another thing too since we're talking about, so we're talking about smart people.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:28.320
How did you guys feel about the questions themselves and the relevancy of this now 30 years later?

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:34.520
I mean, you could definitely feel that it was, you know, made in, you know, 1988.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:48.520
But I also was impressed that, or just surprised that they didn't make them like all like really kind of general categories that would be like, that kids would always get.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.060
Like there were some specific, they felt like real Jeopardy!

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:59.880
categories. Yeah, I would also say that it sort of has the, it's definitely not as difficult as TV Jeopardy!, at least from what I've seen.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.520
But it does have a variation in difficulty.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:12.700
Like you can definitely tell that the $500 question or answer, I guess, is a bit more difficult than the $100.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:14.640
So I think it's got a pretty good balance.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.120
And a lot of the categories are timeless.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:24.320
A lot of them aren't, but there's enough here where somebody in 2020 could still play this game.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.240
All right. And so Joe, you still get control of the board because no one answered.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.980
So what would you like to pick next?

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.160
You know, I'll do play the game for $200.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.980
This gambling feature is hidden behind one clue in the Jeopardy!

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.980
round and two in Double Jeopardy!

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.200
Beep! Sean?

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:47.160
What is Double Jeopardy?

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.160
Oh. Ooh, that is wrong.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.340
I just realized it was listed in the question.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.020
Joe, would you like to respond?

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.400
Man, I wish I watched more Jeopardy!

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:02.700
And we will move on.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.840
We are looking for the Daily Double.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:06.520
Daily Double, yes.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:13.440
The Daily Double. And that gives you the opportunity to wager $1,000 or more or less, to be honest, if you

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.060
didn't feel comfortable with the category, as a way to spice up the game and also a way

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.060
to like completely dominate the game too.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:24.640
Yeah. If you're really feeling good about a category, if you have $3,000 to your name, you can risk

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.140
it all and now jump up to $6,000, which in this kind of game is really going to put you

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:29.960
ahead of the computers.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.440
So how did you guys feel?

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.860
Were the questions tougher with the Daily Doubles?

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.340
Did you bet a lot of money?

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:39.720
How did that work? I did a...

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.120
So now I'm remembering...

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.900
When you said Double Jeopardy, that's what confused me.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.740
Because I was confusing Double Jeopardy with Daily Double.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.300
But I did a true Daily Double when I actually played the game, which just meant I bet everything,

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:57.400
which put me in a substantial lead in the first round, which I thought was very helpful,

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.580
but also could have terribly screwed me if I happened to get a question that I didn't know.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:04.460
It's definitely a risk.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.520
And Sean, were you being risky during your Daily Doubles?

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:15.980
If I was in the lead, I wouldn't bet so much that I could possibly lose the lead.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:21.580
So I tried to make some money, but I didn't go all in like that.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:24.220
Got it. And for the record, I never went all in either.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:30.860
It's tough because you have to bet the amount of money you want before you ever see the question.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.040
Yeah. So it's like, no matter how...

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:40.500
The categories are just vague enough that I don't feel too confident about the general curriculum that might be involved.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:45.440
For sure. Okay, so Joe, you still get to pick another category.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.500
And I'm going to stay with Play the Game for $300.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:54.560
A class or division of people or things regarded as having particular shared characteristics.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.880
Beep, beep. That is Joe.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.520
Joe? What is a category?

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:02.500
That is correct.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:03.740
What is a category?

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:10.660
Okay. And so we've already talked about the varying skill levels of categories, but what were some...

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:18.100
Can you recall anything that came to mind as like a category that either you particularly were good at or that you avoided?

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:27.320
Because I had one category or a recurring theme of just like, you know, things that start with B or things that start with C.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:30.040
Like you would see that and they would have it for every kind of letter.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:37.040
And I thought that was funny that like, it seemed kind of just like a, well, now we can just do anything, but as long as it starts with this letter, we're covered.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:38.880
I kind of like those.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:49.700
I usually stayed away from the ones that were definitely more of the time or ones that were about a certain period, like the 50s.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.720
Like I know very little about the culture of the 50s.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.100
So I would stray away from those.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:10.840
Yeah. I noticed that there were, I mean, I guess, I don't know why I wasn't expecting them to include these little things that are just in jeopardy all the time, but they would have categories that were like, ended up, the answers always ended up being like a play on words for what the category was called.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:24.240
Which I guess, I don't know why I just didn't expect the, like this NES game to do that, to be so similar to, to actual jeopardy, which is weird in retrospect.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.340
And they would have some interesting ones too.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.680
I mean, there was a whole category on Disney.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:35.060
I don't know if you guys got that one, but that seems like very approachable.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:46.520
And then at the same time, you could have right next to it, a whole category called like thick and thin, which, you know, like, which is, which is completely like, what could that be?

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.680
And it goes through a whole gambit of different things, like what is flower?

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.140
What is thin air?

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.340
Like it's very tough to, to tell what those categories are going to be like.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:58.940
So the game keeps you on your toes for sure.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:01.980
Mm-hmm. All right.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.840
So Joe, since you got that one, you're now in the lead with 300 and you get to pick again.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:14.260
Um, I'm going to, I'm going to just stick with play the game, keeping us consistent in the chapters here, uh, for 500.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:20.760
This round features a single clue and requires contestants to wager their money before receiving the question.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:22.740
Beep. Joe.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:25.260
What is final jeopardy?

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:28.100
You are correct again for 500.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:40.620
And let's talk about that final jeopardy because, uh, I think that is the make or break moment because it gives the computers a huge chance to come back at you if you're not prepared with an answer.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.680
So how did you guys feel about the final jeopardy compared to the rest of the game?

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:51.360
Well, in one of my games, uh, both of the computers had ended up with negative amounts, so they couldn't even participate.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.020
Uh, so I ended up just wagering $1 and won anyway.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:10.900
But, um, uh, there was another game where it was actually close and, uh, no, I mean, it's, and especially if you're playing this, how it was probably designed to be made, uh, to be played with, which is with people.

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:18.020
So, uh, there's this thing where you'd have to go into the other room and make sure you couldn't see whoever's typing.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.820
And, um, but I think it, it works.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:28.260
Yeah. I found that really funny the way that they deal with like, make sure you don't look at the screen while your other player is typing.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.620
I thought that was really funny because there's just no other way around it.

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:41.540
But, uh, but yeah, in my, in my game, I had, I, it actually, I had a lead in the first round and it actually ended up being pretty close, uh, by the end though.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:52.340
And final jeopardy is what gave me the, the, the boost, uh, to win, but it is a little bit of, you know, I mean, it's jeopardy rules, but like a little bit of that gamble of like, how much, how much are they going to wager?

00:11:52.340 --> 00:12:01.500
The person who's closest to me, like I want to wager enough that I'll win, but not too much that, you know, hopefully if I get it wrong, I'm still above, you know, above their score.

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:12.120
Yeah. I had one particular final jeopardy where I was in the lead for most of the game and then actually wound up being like 400 under, uh, one of the computer players.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:22.100
And so I did the math and everything for like how much it would take me to make sure that like, you know, well, I can't bet at all, but I also kind of need to like, how much should I put in and stuff like that.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:29.180
And I did all that and the computer wound up giving like a bogus answer, you know, one of those nonsensical ones.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:37.580
And then like also just put in like what seemed to be a completely arbitrary amount, like had no idea that I was only 400 behind them.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:41.140
So like, I don't think that the computer cares enough to do the math.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.780
So I don't know why we would, we would care.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:47.060
All right, Joe.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:51.220
Uh, I'll actually take trust your mechanic for 500.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:54.280
Okay. Trust your mechanic for 500.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:58.840
This mechanic allows for additional players to join in on the fun of Jeopardy.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.860
Beep. Sean.

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.040
What is multiplayer?

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:05.880
You are correct for 500.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.360
That's right. And, uh, that puts you up from the negative.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.600
So you are now at 300.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:14.880
Nice. And, uh, would you like to talk about, uh, the multiplayer?

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:18.060
It sounds like neither you or Joe played multiplayer.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:19.940
I know you attempted to with me.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:25.620
Yeah. We, uh, we, we tried to get some kind of net play going, but that didn't end up working out.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:34.580
Um, but I imagine this being, you know, a poor man's Jackbox now, or like, I don't know if there is a Jackbox game that's just trivia.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:40.520
They're usually much more goofy with it, but it, yeah, it's like a, it's a party game.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.880
It's one of the first party games we played.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:45.940
Yeah. And I think, go ahead.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:54.700
I was going to say, I think that I was surprised at how, even though I didn't actually play it multiplayer, I mean, this functions like Jeopardy.

00:13:54.820 --> 00:14:00.220
I mean, there's a few little bit of, you know, annoyance to it now where you got to type things in and whatnot.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:07.340
But like, I can't see you playing this with a friend and feeling like, oh, that wasn't, that didn't feel like a good trivia game.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.500
Like if you're playing it with another person, like it's just trivia.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:11.460
Like it really just kind of works.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:17.620
Yeah. Me and my fiance did play this, uh, this game as multiplayer.

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:20.180
And I have a couple of interesting observations.

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:27.620
And the most interesting actually is that this game is technically the first three player NES game.

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:38.980
Because you're, you are able to, uh, use controller one for both player one and three, but player two needs to have a second controller.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:45.400
So you, if you only have one NES controller in your house, you're like out of luck to play this multiplayer game.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:51.180
Like it still somehow finds a way to lock you behind it, even though you could theoretically play with just one controller.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:52.560
Yeah. That's interesting.

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:57.480
Because I would have thought that the only reason to do that is to make sure people are still buying a second controller.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.980
But didn't the NES come with two controllers?

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:04.440
Yeah. The NES didn't, uh, did come with two controllers for the most part.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.420
Um, like with the action set and stuff like that.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:10.640
But I imagine, you know, you could buy this console in many different packs.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:15.280
Like there's that pack that came with Rob and there's that pack that came with the Zapper.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:19.040
So, I mean, I'm sure there was eventually a version sold with just one controller.

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:28.740
Huh. The Famicom, on the other hand though, uh, as long as it wasn't the Famicom disc system, the original Famicom, the two controllers always came with it because they were built into the system.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:30.280
Oh, I see.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.680
No plugging. All right.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:37.320
Uh, so Sean, since you got that one right, you can choose the next category.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:40.560
Let's do Trust Your Mechanic for 400, Alex.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:47.820
In a game of real Jeopardy, sometimes this mechanic causes contestants to be unable to answer all questions on the board.

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:50.120
Beep? Is that a beep?

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:52.100
Okay, that's a beep.

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:53.260
What do you got, Joe?

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.320
Uh, time limit?

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.020
What is a time limit?

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.380
I'll allow it because I didn't correct you in time.

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:04.760
But yes, don't forget to always say what or who.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:08.360
Yes, what is a time limit?

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:19.480
Actually, there's no time limit in the NES version of the game, so you're forced to answer every single question in both the first round and Double Jeopardy.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:21.040
How did you guys feel about that?

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.840
I think that was a little too many questions for one game.

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:25.740
I was kind of feeling that way too.

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:28.160
Maybe, I mean, you played with someone else.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.680
Maybe if I played with someone else, I would have felt differently.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:35.420
But when I was playing against a computer, there was a point where I was like, come on, please tell me they're going to cut me off at some point.

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:36.980
We were going to have to go through all these questions.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:42.240
Are you saying in a real game of Jeopardy, there's an overall timer that's going on the entire time?

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:47.040
I mean, I know it's like, you know, for commercials and everything, but it's like, it's displayed?

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:53.140
No, it's not displayed, but it is, it is for, it resets at the end of each round.

00:16:53.140 --> 00:17:01.340
But essentially, because they need commercial breaks and stuff like that, there's only a certain amount of time that you get during single Jeopardy and double Jeopardy.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:05.540
And so very, it's very rare that they actually answer all the questions on the board.

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:08.760
Okay. Because there are timers within the questions.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:13.780
Like you can only buzz in for 10 seconds and you have like a minute to write out your answer.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:20.600
